WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.840
All right friends.

00:04.840 --> 00:11.200
So so far we have learned that the SQL server can make its own decisions on how to execute your queries.

00:11.200 --> 00:14.320
And the SQL makes those plans based on the statistics.

00:14.320 --> 00:20.920
But sometimes the plan that you are getting from the database might be not the best one for your query,

00:21.000 --> 00:23.520
and there could be many reasons why this could happen.

00:23.520 --> 00:30.440
Maybe the statistics are not up to date, or you have a lot of indexes and the database engine get confused.

00:30.440 --> 00:34.600
And here exactly where we need the SQL hints.

00:34.600 --> 00:42.800
So you can use the SQL hints in order to command to force the SQL database on how exactly your SQL query

00:42.840 --> 00:48.800
should be executed, so you can intervene and change the steps in the execution plan.

00:48.840 --> 00:50.360
So let's see how we can do that.

00:50.400 --> 00:50.760
All right.

00:50.760 --> 00:52.360
So now let's have a very simple query.

00:52.400 --> 00:55.240
We are just joining the table orders with the customers.

00:55.280 --> 00:57.200
And we are showing like few columns.

00:57.240 --> 01:02.800
Now if you go and execute it and we go and check the execution plan, we can see in this plan that it

01:02.800 --> 01:08.110
is using the clustered index in order to read the data from the orders and the customers, and then

01:08.110 --> 01:11.390
it is using the nested loop in order to do the joins.

01:11.430 --> 01:13.630
Now let's see that our tables are really big.

01:13.630 --> 01:16.910
But still the SQL is using the nested loops.

01:16.910 --> 01:19.430
And of course this is not good for large tables.

01:19.430 --> 01:23.950
And maybe the SQL was confused with the indexes and statistics and so on.

01:23.950 --> 01:26.310
And it decided to use the nested loops.

01:26.310 --> 01:33.150
So now in order to force the SQL to use another type of join, we can go and give a hint in our query

01:33.150 --> 01:36.590
for the SQL to use different types for the join.

01:36.590 --> 01:37.990
So let's go and do that.

01:38.030 --> 01:40.630
We're going to go at the end of our query.

01:40.630 --> 01:42.350
And we're going to say option.

01:42.670 --> 01:47.510
And inside it we're going to say use the hash join like this.

01:47.750 --> 01:48.990
So that's it.

01:48.990 --> 01:50.190
This is our query.

01:50.230 --> 01:54.150
And at the end we are giving the database a hint for the execution plan.

01:54.150 --> 01:56.070
So let's go and try that out.

01:56.070 --> 01:57.910
So let's check the execution plan.

01:57.910 --> 02:01.910
And now as you can see SQL is using different type of join.

02:01.910 --> 02:05.070
So with that we are intervening in the execution plan.

02:05.070 --> 02:11.550
Learn, and we are making choices so that we have changed that technicality on how the SQL is joining

02:11.550 --> 02:12.550
those two tables.

02:12.590 --> 02:12.910
All right.

02:12.910 --> 02:14.550
So now let's go and change something else.

02:14.550 --> 02:20.630
Like for example, instead of having index scan I would like to have an index seek.

02:20.670 --> 02:27.070
So if you have the right index in your table, you can go and tell SQL how to read your data in the

02:27.070 --> 02:27.550
table.

02:27.550 --> 02:29.230
So let's go and do that.

02:29.270 --> 02:33.630
Currently here we have an index scan on the table customers.

02:33.630 --> 02:35.870
So we can go over here near the table.

02:35.870 --> 02:37.470
And we're going to say width.

02:37.510 --> 02:41.310
And inside it we're going to say for SQL force seek.

02:41.350 --> 02:44.270
So we are forcing a SQL to use the Seek index.

02:44.270 --> 02:50.990
So we can use those keywords near the table in order to specify for SQL how to load the data.

02:50.990 --> 02:55.230
If you are not specifying anything like here with the orders, we don't have here any hints.

02:55.230 --> 03:01.190
That means we are counting on the execution plan that is generated from the SQL.

03:01.230 --> 03:06.670
But if you don't want the recommendations, you can go and specify which one should be used.

03:06.710 --> 03:08.820
So now let's go and execute it.

03:08.860 --> 03:14.660
Now we got an error because the SQL is not able to process what we are asking for.

03:14.700 --> 03:17.580
And I think maybe we are using the force command as well.

03:17.580 --> 03:18.500
The hash join.

03:18.540 --> 03:21.940
Let me just uncomment this and let's go and give it another try.

03:21.980 --> 03:23.540
And now it is working.

03:23.540 --> 03:25.300
So let's go to the execution plan.

03:25.340 --> 03:27.660
So you can see we got again the nested loop.

03:27.700 --> 03:32.620
And now if you go to the customers table you can see now it is using the index seq.

03:32.660 --> 03:35.660
So it is not using anymore the index scan.

03:35.700 --> 03:40.300
So as you can see again we are intervening and forcing a SQL to use the method.

03:40.300 --> 03:42.540
That might be better for our query.

03:42.580 --> 03:49.500
Now if you are creating a lot of indexes in one table and the SQL is still not targeting the right index.

03:49.500 --> 03:53.460
So if you check the object, you can see it is targeting specific index.

03:53.460 --> 04:00.260
But if you have a better index than that, you can give a hint for the SQL to use a specific index.

04:00.340 --> 04:02.140
And we can do that like this.

04:02.180 --> 04:06.860
If you go over here and remove the force seq and you say use index.

04:06.860 --> 04:10.050
And then we have to go and specify the index name.

04:10.050 --> 04:12.970
So let's go and get again the primary key over here.

04:13.010 --> 04:18.850
Now I'm telling SQL you have to go and use this index in order to scan the table customers.

04:18.890 --> 04:20.730
So let's go and try this out.

04:20.770 --> 04:25.850
And if you go to the execution plan you can see it is as well targeting this index.

04:25.930 --> 04:34.370
So not only can force SQL for specific type of loading or joining, you can force SQL to use a specific

04:34.410 --> 04:35.810
index that you created.

04:35.850 --> 04:36.770
All right friends.

04:36.770 --> 04:39.610
So as you can see SQL hints are very powerful.

04:39.610 --> 04:45.530
But we have to be very careful with them because I really had a bad experience using them in my projects.

04:45.570 --> 04:46.970
So here are my recommendations.

04:46.970 --> 04:48.010
And what happened.

04:48.050 --> 04:53.610
So what could happen is that you are optimizing the performance in the development database, and you

04:53.610 --> 04:54.890
start using the hands.

04:54.890 --> 04:56.690
And the speed was really good.

04:56.730 --> 05:03.490
And once you roll that out to another database, the production database, this hint will not be working

05:03.490 --> 05:04.250
correctly.

05:04.250 --> 05:08.370
The same hint that you are using might not improve the performance.

05:08.370 --> 05:14.290
And one reason is that sometimes the productive predictive database has like large data compared to

05:14.330 --> 05:16.050
the development database.

05:16.050 --> 05:20.330
So you have really to test the hint in each database that you have.

05:20.330 --> 05:25.850
So if your hint is working in one environment, that doesn't mean it's going to work in the other one.

05:25.850 --> 05:27.490
So always make sure to test.

05:27.490 --> 05:33.210
And the second recommendation that don't use the hint as a permanent fix for your queries.

05:33.210 --> 05:38.290
So what this means let's say that you are working in the project and one of your queries are very slow.

05:38.330 --> 05:44.850
Now, if it's not clear why the execution plan is really bad, you can go and use the hints as a workaround

05:44.850 --> 05:48.970
in order to speed up your query again, but it still has a workaround.

05:49.010 --> 05:49.770
Temporary.

05:49.810 --> 05:55.170
You still have to invest and spend time in order to analyze what was the root cause.

05:55.170 --> 05:59.330
So maybe it is an old statistics or you have wrong indexing and so on.

05:59.330 --> 06:05.570
So use hints only to work around and speed up your queries, but don't use it as a permanent fix.

06:05.570 --> 06:11.970
So friends, SQL hints are really amazing in order to control the execution plan, but use it very carefully

06:11.970 --> 06:14.570
and only if there is like an emergency.
